 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "CONNEMARA. A wild, wet, wonderful corner of Galway: bog, lakes, mountains and sky, in that order of area. About half of it is sky, and the sky has opinions."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE TWELVE BENS. Na Beanna Beola, a ring of quartzite peaks, the tallest at 729 metres. In clear weather you can see for forty miles. 'Clear weather' is a rumour."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "KYLEMORE ABBEY. A Victorian castle built in the 1860s by Mitchell Henry for his wife Margaret. Since 1920 it has been home to Benedictine nuns, who keep a very fine walled garden."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "TURF. Cut from the bog and dried in stacks, burnt for heat for centuries. Connemara bogs are protected now. The old sods smell of peat, smoke and a good yarn."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "STONE WALLS. A thousand miles of them across Connemara, all put up by hand with no mortar. The gaps are on purpose. The wind needs to go through or it takes the wall."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "connemara_alcock_brown",
   "title": "Alcock and Brown",
   "text": "On 15 June 1919 John Alcock and Arthur Brown landed in Derrygimlagh bog, near Clifden, after the first non-stop flight across the Atlantic. Their Vickers Vimy nosed over in the soft bog. Nearby, Marconi's wireless station had been sending messages to Canada since 1907."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 2,
   "plaque": "connemara_killary",
   "title": "Killary Harbour",
   "text": "North of here, Killary Harbour is a long, narrow sea inlet carved by glaciers, often called Ireland's fjord. It forms the border between Galway and Mayo. Ropes of mussels hang from rafts in its sheltered water, and the mountains drop straight into the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "arts_sean_nos",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Sean-n\u00f3s",
   "text": "Sean-n\u00f3s, 'the old style', is singing in Irish without instruments, one voice, a long story and more ornament than a cathedral. Connemara, and Carna above all, is its heartland, and Seosamh \u00d3 h\u00c9ana\u00ed of Carna, Joe Heaney, born in 1919, carried it to Dublin, Glasgow, London and New York before he died in 1984. Every autumn at the Oireachtas, the Irish-language festival founded in 1897, the singers compete for Corn U\u00ed Riada, the cup named for the composer Se\u00e1n \u00d3 Riada. A good sean-n\u00f3s singer can hold a pub silent. A great one can hold it crying."
  }
 ],
